// @run
// CONTRACT: a union BOX carrying a literal-union arm answers `is` identically whether the
// receiver's annotation names the box by a DECLARED ALIAS (`type W = K | i32`) or spells it
// INLINE (`K | i32`) — and the answer is MEMBERSHIP over the payload, so a tested SUB-ALIAS
// answers NO for a member of the arm that is not in the tested set.
//
// THE ALIAS SPELLING WAS A SILENT WRONG ANSWER AND THE INLINE SPELLING WAS CORRECT. Every
// predicate that decides "does this box carry a literal-union arm" answers by splitting the
// SPELLING it is handed at top-level `|`, and an alias splits to one atom (`"W"`), so the
// alias-annotated receiver fell past the membership arm into the box-tag compare — whose
// `tag == 2` is TRUE for every string the box holds, members and non-members alike, with
// `vl check` rc 0 and `vl build` clean. The box-tag exemption it landed on ("one string-repped
// arm, so the tag decides exactly") is sound for the FULL arm and not for a proper subset of
// it, which is what makes the tested sub-alias the cell that exposes it.
//
// MEASURED: the alias-annotated receiver now emits a body BYTE-IDENTICAL to its
// inline-annotated twin (19 instructions, `wasm-tools print`), which is the strongest
// statement of the contract — the two spellings are not merely equivalent, they are one
// lowering.

type K = "a" | "b"
type KS = "a"

// ── the alias spelling and the inline spelling, side by side ────────────────────
// @log alias:Y
// @log alias:N
type W = K | i32
function viaAlias(u: W) {
  if u is KS { return "alias:Y" }
  return "alias:N"
}
print(viaAlias("a"))
print(viaAlias("b"))

// @log inline:Y
// @log inline:N
function viaInline(u: K | i32) {
  if u is KS { return "inline:Y" }
  return "inline:N"
}
print(viaInline("a"))
print(viaInline("b"))

// The i32 arm answers NO to the string membership through both spellings.
// @log alias:N
// @log inline:N
print(viaAlias(7))
print(viaInline(7))

// The FULL arm as the tested type still answers YES for both members — the exemption's
// own case, which must not move.
// @log full:Y
// @log full:Y
// @log full:N
function testFull(u: W) {
  if u is K { return "full:Y" }
  return "full:N"
}
print(testFull("a"))
print(testFull("b"))
print(testFull(7))

// ── the alias whose other arm is a plain `string`: TWO string-repped arms, where the
// tag cannot discriminate at all and the membership arm is the only lowering ─────
// @log wstr:Y
// @log wstr:N
type WS = K | string
function viaAliasStr(u: WS) {
  if u is KS { return "wstr:Y" }
  return "wstr:N"
}
print(viaAliasStr("a"))
print(viaAliasStr("zz"))

// ── two litunion arms under one alias ──────────────────────────────────────────
// @log two:Y
// @log two:N
type K2 = "c" | "d"
type WB = K | K2
function viaTwoArms(u: WB) {
  if u is KS { return "two:Y" }
  return "two:N"
}
print(viaTwoArms("a"))
print(viaTwoArms("c"))

// EVERY CELL IN THIS FILE COMPILES BEFORE THE FIX AND ANSWERS WRONG, which is deliberate:
// the failure mode being pinned is a SILENT one, so the file has to redden on a log mismatch
// rather than on a diagnostic. The alias spellings that instead failed LOUDLY before the fix
// live in `is-litunion-arm-box-alias-loud-spellings.vl` so they cannot mask this one.
